CSS LINEAR-GRADIENT
==================

linear-gradient is a value of
background-image.

It creates a smooth transition
between two or more colors
in a straight line.

No image file is required.


BASIC SYNTAX
============

background-image: linear-gradient(direction, color1, color2);

Example:
.box {
    background-image: linear-gradient(red, blue);
}


DEFAULT BEHAVIOR
================

If direction is not specified:

→ Gradient flows from TOP to BOTTOM

Equivalent to:
linear-gradient(to bottom, red, blue);


DIRECTION OPTIONS
=================

to top
→ bottom → top

to bottom
→ top → bottom

to left
→ right → left

to right
→ left → right


Examples:
.box {
    background-image: linear-gradient(to right, red, blue);
}

.box {
    background-image: linear-gradient(to top, yellow, green);
}


ANGLE BASED DIRECTION
=====================

You can use degrees instead of keywords.

0deg   → bottom to top
90deg  → left to right
180deg → top to bottom
270deg → right to left

Example:
.box {
    background-image: linear-gradient(45deg, red, blue);
}


MULTIPLE COLORS
===============

You can add more than two colors.

Example:
.box {
    background-image: linear-gradient(to right, red, orange, yellow);
}


VERTICAL GRADIENT
=================

Example:
.box {
    background-image: linear-gradient(to bottom, lightblue, navy);
}


IMPORTANT NOTES
===============

linear-gradient is treated as an image.
So it works with:

→ background-size
→ background-position
→ background-repeat


COMMON MISTAKES
===============

- Forgetting height of element
- Expecting gradient to affect layout
- Using background-color instead of background-image


BEST PRACTICE
=============

Always set height
when practicing gradients.

.box {
    height: 200px;
    background-image: linear-gradient(to right, red, blue);
}
